{
  "id": "GP03Z",
  "version": "2.0",
  "title": "Colonoscopy",
  "description": "Colonoscopy is a procedure where your doctor uses a long, flexible camera to examine your entire large intestine (colon and rectum). This important screening and diagnostic tool allows your doctor to look for polyps, tumors, inflammation, bleeding sources, and other abnormalities. Before the procedure, you'll prepare your colon by drinking a special cleaning solution to clear it completely. During the procedure, you'll be sedated to keep you comfortable while your doctor gently advances the camera through your colon. If your doctor finds polyps or areas of concern, they can remove tissue samples (biopsies) or remove growths during the same procedure. Most colonoscopies take 15-30 minutes, though therapeutic procedures may take longer. The cost of this procedure may vary depending on the complexity involved. Categories include diagnostic, simple, diagnostic, complex (eus, biopsy), therapeutic, simple, and therapeutic, mucosal resection.",
